+A hookimpl argument which declares a default value now receives the value passed by the hook call, falling back to the hookspec's default and only then to the hookimpl's own default. Previously the hookimpl's default was always used.
--- a/pluggy/docs/index.rst
+++ b/pluggy/docs/index.rst
@@ -639,6 +639,34 @@
     pm.hook.myhook(config=config, args=args)
     # New caller; hookimpls will get new_arg="get this".
     pm.hook.myhook(config=config, args=args, new_arg="get this")
+
+
+.. _hookimpl_arg_default:
+
+Hookimpl argument defaults
+^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
+A *hookimpl* may also declare a default value for an argument. The value a
+*hookimpl* receives for such an argument is, in order of precedence:
+
+1. the value passed by the hook call,
+2. the default value declared by the *hookspec*,
+3. the default value declared by the *hookimpl* itself.
+
+The *hookimpl*'s own default is therefore only used when neither the call nor
+the *hookspec* provides the argument, for example when a new *hookimpl* runs
+against an older *hookspec* which does not have the argument yet:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    @hookimpl
+    def myhook(config, args, new_arg="fallback"):
+        pass
+
+This applies to plain *hookimpls* as well as to wrappers.
+
+.. versionchanged:: 1.7
+   Previously the *hookimpl*'s default was always used, even when the hook
+   call passed a value for the argument.
 
 
